            {
              "_key": "c6l3-006",
              "_type": "callout",
              "type": "insight",
              "text": "Notice the last column. The ₹93,600 Rajeev deducts on rent and the flat purchase is never his credit. It belongs to his landlord and to the seller. Mixing up the two is one of the most common errors at filing time."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c6l3-007",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c6l3-007s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "April to December: Cruising on Autopilot",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c6l3-008",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c6l3-008s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "His employer projects ₹22,00,000 for the year, subtracts the ₹75,000 standard deduction, and computes tax of ₹2,40,500 on ₹21,25,000. Spread over twelve months, that is about ₹20,042 a month. Meanwhile SBI, the company deposit and the dividend-paying company quietly deduct their 10%, and nobody involved knows about anyone else.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c6l3-009",
              "_type": "mathBlock",
              "latex": "\\text{Salary TDS, April to December} = 20{,}042 \\times 9 = 1{,}80{,}375",
              "caption": "Based on salary alone, because that is all payroll knows"
            },
            {
              "_key": "c6l3-010",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c6l3-010s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "December: He Becomes a Deductor",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c6l3-011",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c6l3-011s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "On 15 December 2026 Rajeev registers his new flat. The agreed price is ₹75,00,000, but the stamp duty value is ₹78,00,000, and TDS is charged on the higher figure. He deducts 1%, ₹78,000, pays the seller the balance, and files Form 141 with his own PAN by 30 January 2027. No TAN is needed. He then downloads Form 132 and hands it to the seller.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c6l3-012",
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "quiz",
              "title": "Quick Check: Whose Credit Is the ₹78,000?",
              "question": "Rajeev deducted ₹78,000 when buying the flat and deposited it with the government. Who claims that ₹78,000 as a tax credit?",
              "options": [
                "Rajeev, in his own return",
                "The seller, against the capital gains tax on the sale",
                "Nobody, it is a transaction tax",
                "Rajeev's employer, by reducing his salary TDS"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "The seller earned the income, so the credit is the seller's. Rajeev is only the collecting agent. His obligations are to deduct correctly on the higher of price and stamp duty value, deposit on time through Form 141, and issue Form 132. If he claims it himself, the claim will be disallowed and the seller will be left chasing a missing credit."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c6l3-013",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c6l3-013s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "January: The Declaration That Fixes the Year",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c6l3-014",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c6l3-014s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "In January, Rajeev finally does the sum he should have done in April. His interest and dividends add ₹1,36,000 to his income, on which only ₹13,600 has been deducted at 10%, while his marginal rate is 25%. He gives payroll a Form 122 declaring the other income and the TDS already suffered.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
