            {
              "_key": "c6l3-024",
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "scenario",
              "title": "Scenario: The Version Where He Said Nothing",
              "scenario": "Suppose Rajeev had never filed Form 122. His employer would have deducted ₹2,40,500 for the year based on salary alone, and the bank, company and dividend payer ₹13,600 between them.",
              "prompt": "How much would he owe at filing, and what else would it cost him?",
              "modelAnswer": "Total TDS would be ₹2,54,100 against a true tax of ₹2,75,860, leaving ₹21,760 to pay as self-assessment tax. Because that is well above ₹10,000, advance tax was due during the year, so interest under Sections 424 and 425 (formerly 234B and 234C) would be added on top, running at 1% a month on the shortfall and on the missed instalments. He would also be filing with a payment to make rather than a clean nil balance, and would have had to fund the amount in one go in July. The lesson is not that the tax changed. It did not. What changed is when it was collected and whether interest was attached. A single form in April, or even in January, is the difference."
            },
